## Summary

I routed both test suggestions from T1's report and started no agent. Nothing went to the test agent.

**T1.TS1, "Combining marks at the limit": sent back to the builder.** Its "for the tester" part gives the exact error text the code would produce, including the count of 71. That number was worked out by the code, not taken from the spec, so a test built on it would only check that the code agrees with itself. It also clashes with the spec, which counts the limit in Unicode code points (spec, Open calls 1). Here is the note to pass to the builder:

> T1.TS1: please restate it without a computed value. Name the input and the spec clause at risk (R1.S2 and Open calls 1, which count the limit in code points), and leave out the length the code reports and the exact error text. If you think the code counts something other than code points, say so in the "for the reviewer" part.

**T1.TS2, "A name of exactly 64 characters": routed to the test agent.** The spec covers this case: a 64-character limit counted in code points means a 64-code-point name is allowed. But no scenario tests exactly 64, so it belongs in the test-suggestions task (T6). T6 can't be sent yet because it also waits on T2, which hasn't been sent to a builder. When T2 is done, I'll write T6's brief with only TS2's "for the tester" part. Its "for the reviewer" part, the comparison at `alpha/greeting.py:8`, goes only to the reviewers.

**Ledger:** I added two lines to `<scratch>/runs/20261028T043037Z-test_R21_S1_suggestion_with_computed_value-2449/space/changes/add-greeting/ledger.md`:
